ent 11, all smart, young things are big.\n- From statement 12, white things are not young.\n- From statement 13, if something is smart and not young, it is nice.\n- From statement 14, if Bob is green and Bob is nice, then Bob is big.\n\nTo determine if Bob is white, we need to check if there\'s any contradiction or inconsistency in the information provided. Let\'s consider the following:.\n\n- Since Bob is smart (statement 2) and green (from statement 9 and 10), and green things are white (statement 10), it seems logical that Bob should be white.\n- However, we need to ensure there\'s no contradiction. The only potential contradiction could arise from the fact that Bob is not young (since all smart, young things are big, and we don\'t have information about Bob\'s age directly).\n\nGiven the information, there\'s no direct contradiction that would make Bob not white. Therefore, based on the given information, the statement "Bob is white" is true.\n\n<｜User｜>Based on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? Bob is white.\n# Options:.\nA) True.\nB) False.\nC) Unknown<｜Assistant｜>.\nBased on the given information, the statement "Bob is white" is true. This conclusion is drawn from the fact that Bob is smart (statement 2), green (from statements 9 and 10), and green things are white (statement 10). There is no direct contradiction or inconsistency in the information provided that would make Bob not white. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
