s furry.** (This tells us that if Bob is white, it must also be furry.).\n15. **All red, rough things are big.** (This tells us that if something is red, rough, and big, it must be big.).\n16. **All rough things are smart.** (This tells us that if something is rough, it must also be smart.).\n17. **If Fiona is furry and Fiona is white then Fiona is smart.** (This tells us that if Fiona is furry and white, it must also be smart.).\n\nNow, let\'s consider the statement "Bob is not white." We need to check if there is any information that directly or indirectly contradicts this statement.\n\nFrom the given information, we know:.\n- Bob is red.\n- All red things are furry.\n- All furry things are red.\n- All red things are rough.\n- All rough things are smart.\n- If Bob is white, then Bob is furry.\n\nSince Bob is red, it cannot be white because all red things are red, not white. Therefore, the statement "Bob is not white" is true based on the given information.\n\n<｜User｜>Based on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? Bob is not white.\n# Options:.\nA) True.\nB) False.\nC) Unknown<｜Assistant｜>.\nBased on the given information, the statement "Bob is not white" is true. This conclusion is drawn from the fact that Bob is red, and all red things are red, not white. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
