n Is Not Red**: This gives us a color description of the lion.\n11. **Squirrel Chases Bear**: This shows that the squirrel is chasing the bear.\n12. **Squirrel Chases Dog**: This implies that the squirrel is chasing the dog.\n13. **Squirrel Is Green**: This provides a color description of the squirrel.\n14. **Squirrel Is Round**: This describes the shape of the squirrel.\n15. **Squirrel Is Young**: This indicates the age of the squirrel.\n16. **Squirrel Sees Dog**: This means the squirrel can see the dog.\n17. **If Something Visits Bear Then It Sees Bear**: This rule states that if something visits the bear, it will see the bear.\n18. **If Something Sees Bear Then Bear Sees Dog**: This rule states that if something sees the bear, the bear will see the dog.\n19. **If Something Visits Dog and Dog is Young Then Dog Sees Bear**: This rule states that if something visits the dog and the dog is young, the dog will see the bear.\n20. **If Something Is Green Then It Sees Dog**: This rule states that if something is green, it will see the dog.\n21. **If Something Sees Dog Then It Visits Bear**: This rule states that if something sees the dog, it will visit the bear.\n\nNow, let\'s evaluate the statement "The lion does not visit the bear":.\n\n- From rule 18, if something sees the bear, the bear sees the dog. Since the lion does not see the bear (rule 10), the bear does not see the dog.\n- From rule 19, if something visits the dog and the dog is young, the dog sees the bear. Since the dog is young (rule 15), the dog sees the bear.\n- From rule 20, if something is green, it sees the dog. Since the lion is not green (rule 9), the lion does not see the dog.\n- From rule 21, if something sees the dog, it visits the bear. Since the lion does not see the dog, the lion does not visit the bear. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
